1
00:00:19,227 --> 00:00:20,770
Feelin' alive!

2
00:00:21,395 --> 00:00:24,482
- Agent Lisbon?
- Hey. Patrick Jane, consultant.

3
00:00:25,399 --> 00:00:26,526
- Hello.
- Morning, sheriff.

4
00:00:26,651 --> 00:00:29,320
Hi. Trey Hughes.
Thanks for coming. It's, uh...

5
00:00:29,487 --> 00:00:31,864
- Tell us about the victim.
- Look, hawk.

6
00:00:32,114 --> 00:00:33,449
Family of haw... oh, they're gone.

7
00:00:33,574 --> 00:00:36,494
- Rick Loomis, been in town five months.
- Wow.

8
00:00:36,702 --> 00:00:38,538
Dead in his truck by the creek.

9
00:00:38,829 --> 00:00:40,331
Well, maybe he died of happiness.

10
00:00:40,957 --> 00:00:43,876
Too much natural beauty,
It's the Stendhal Syndrome.

11
00:00:44,877 --> 00:00:46,796
- No. He was shot in the head.
- No?

12
00:00:47,004 --> 00:00:48,381
Oh, well. Never mind.

13
00:00:49,799 --> 00:00:50,841
Beautiful!

14
00:00:51,425 --> 00:00:53,177
Who found the body
all the way out here?

15
00:00:53,803 --> 00:00:57,390
Another prospector saw
Loomis' body in the truck yesterday

16
00:00:57,515 --> 00:01:00,643
thought he was sleeping.
Then she came back this morning

17
00:01:00,810 --> 00:01:03,145
and saw him, and checked
and saw he was shot dead.

18
00:01:03,479 --> 00:01:05,690
- A prospector, you say?
- Yeah, for gold.

19
00:01:05,982 --> 00:01:09,694
A guy discovered trace gold
in these hills about a year ago

20
00:01:09,819 --> 00:01:11,779
got on the TV news about it.

21
00:01:11,904 --> 00:01:13,864
Folks haven't stopped comin'
ever since.

22
00:01:14,073 --> 00:01:15,950
Like a gold rush.

23
00:01:18,244 --> 00:01:21,038
Yeah. Well, gold's over 1,300 an ounce

24
00:01:21,163 --> 00:01:23,833
and unemployment's at 12%.

25
00:01:24,834 --> 00:01:27,628
Got a lotta hard-nosed,
well-motivated folks here.

26
00:01:27,962 --> 00:01:29,005
I don't blame 'em.

27
00:01:30,047 --> 00:01:31,799
Poor souls gonna be disappointed.

28
00:01:32,091 --> 00:01:33,718
Poor souls? There's no gold?

29
00:01:34,176 --> 00:01:37,263
Oh, yeah, there's still some
in the stream bed. Always has been.

30
00:01:37,638 --> 00:01:41,601
But... it's enough to pay
for your lodging, food

31
00:01:41,767 --> 00:01:43,144
if you work hard to get it

32
00:01:43,436 --> 00:01:45,104
but there's no El Dorado here.

33
00:01:45,521 --> 00:01:47,607
I tell 'em that, but they don't listen.

34
00:01:47,982 --> 00:01:49,025
Murder weapon?

35
00:01:49,650 --> 00:01:50,693
Nothin' yet.

36
00:01:52,361 --> 00:01:55,156
Hey, fellas.
How about you look around for the gun?

37
00:02:01,287 --> 00:02:03,581
- Jane!
- Sorry, sorry, sorry.

38
00:02:16,719 --> 00:02:18,054
Sorry, sorry.

39
00:02:21,015 --> 00:02:22,308
I didn't touch a thing.

40
00:02:24,644 --> 00:02:25,895
Our victim liked smooth jazz

41
00:02:26,020 --> 00:02:28,648
yet his radio station
was tuned to heavy metal

42
00:02:28,939 --> 00:02:30,566
which suggests that the jazz-hating killer

43
00:02:30,691 --> 00:02:34,195
popped out the CD and found
music more to his taste

44
00:02:34,612 --> 00:02:36,489
while he drove the truck over here

45
00:02:36,614 --> 00:02:38,866
probably with the victim
already dead in the back.

46
00:02:39,075 --> 00:02:40,785
I'll have forensics dust for prints.

47
00:02:41,243 --> 00:02:44,622
Excuse me! Excuse me!
Excuse me. Hi.

48
00:02:45,164 --> 00:02:46,290
Hi, Melinda.

49
00:02:47,875 --> 00:02:51,337
That's Melinda.
She's the one who found Loomis' body.

50
00:02:51,921 --> 00:02:53,589
Hi. Melinda Drew. Hi.

51
00:02:53,756 --> 00:02:55,841
Um, you guys aren't gonna
be much longer, are you?

52
00:02:56,008 --> 00:02:57,677
I mean, no disrespect
to the dead fella

53
00:02:57,802 --> 00:02:58,928
but we're losing daylight.

54
00:02:59,095 --> 00:03:02,306
Yeah, we'll do the best we can.
Ma'am, just stay behind the tape.

55
00:03:03,307 --> 00:03:04,684
Now when did you first see the truck?

56
00:03:05,142 --> 00:03:08,562
Uh, truck was here when I started work
yesterday, around 6:00 A.M.

57
00:03:09,021 --> 00:03:10,981
And did Mr. Loomis work
up here often?

58
00:03:11,273 --> 00:03:14,318
Uh, no, I-I haven't seen him
around here in a while.

59
00:03:14,860 --> 00:03:17,113
May we get back to work now, ma'am?
Please?

60
00:03:17,321 --> 00:03:20,700
It'll be soon, I expect.
Just stay behind the yellow tape.

61
00:03:21,283 --> 00:03:22,326
Gold!

62
00:03:23,035 --> 00:03:24,203
Hey, that's my equipment.

63
00:03:24,412 --> 00:03:25,871
- There's more!
- CBI!

64
00:03:25,996 --> 00:03:27,957
- Oh, it's so cool!
- That's mine!

65
00:03:28,082 --> 00:03:29,709
- Relax.
- That gold belongs to me!

66
00:03:29,834 --> 00:03:31,085
Now, we're not gonna do anything...

67
00:03:31,210 --> 00:03:33,879
- That's no fair! They're letting her in!
- No! No, they aren't!

68
00:03:34,004 --> 00:03:36,632
This is a... Back up!
This is a crime scene!

69
00:03:36,882 --> 00:03:37,925
Hey, hey!

70
00:03:39,051 --> 00:03:40,219
Are you insane?

71
00:03:41,220 --> 00:03:44,724
Ow... my ankle.
I'm fine. I'm fine.

72
00:04:02,823 --> 00:04:05,033
- It's red. That's good.
- How can red be good?

73
00:04:05,159 --> 00:04:08,287
Well, blue means torn ligaments
and red means it's a sprain.

74
00:04:08,412 --> 00:04:09,621
Sprain can be as bad as a break.

75
00:04:09,955 --> 00:04:12,541
{\an8}It's not sprained. Let's focus here.

76
00:04:12,750 --> 00:04:15,669
{\an8}- Job, murder, talk to me.
- Rick Loomis, 43.

77
00:04:15,794 --> 00:04:17,921
{\an8}Moved to Hadley six months ago
with his son and girlfriend.

78
00:04:18,130 --> 00:04:19,882
{\an8}Lives in a trailer home
near the gold panning creek.

79
00:04:20,007 --> 00:04:22,217
{\an8}We got something on the kill shot.
Bullet was from a colt .45.

80
00:04:22,468 --> 00:04:24,928
{\an8}- A colt? How wild west-y.
- Yup.

81
00:04:25,179 --> 00:04:27,931
{\an8}Plus, the lack of blood in the truck bed
means Jane was right.

82
00:04:28,640 --> 00:04:30,726
{\an8}The victim was shot somewhere else
and driven to the creek.

83
00:04:30,934 --> 00:04:32,853
{\an8}- Any prints?
- Only the victim's.

84
00:04:32,978 --> 00:04:35,063
{\an8}He's been dead awhile, the coroner says
two days at least.

85
00:04:35,189 --> 00:04:36,982
{\an8}I'll head back to Hadley,
corral Jane

86
00:04:37,107 --> 00:04:38,859
{\an8}go and see the girlfriend and the son.

87
00:04:39,193 --> 00:04:41,236
{\an8}The guy's gone for two days,
and they don't report him missing?

88
00:04:41,403 --> 00:04:43,155
{\an8}Rigsby and I can go.
You should stay off that ankle.

89
00:04:43,363 --> 00:04:46,825
{\an8}I'm fine. I'll go.
Agent Hightower, hi.

90
00:04:47,201 --> 00:04:48,827
{\an8}Um, I heard you took a fall.

91
00:04:49,453 --> 00:04:50,496
I'm fine.

92
00:04:50,913 --> 00:04:52,706
Ow! I'm fine.

93
00:04:56,502 --> 00:04:58,295
{\an8}So I pull out the topo map

94
00:04:58,545 --> 00:05:00,631
{\an8}there's a creek running
right through the claim.

95
00:05:00,881 --> 00:05:02,174
{\an8}I found a nugget the first day.

96
00:05:02,299 --> 00:05:03,801
{\an8}Yeah. Once you get that first hit

97
00:05:04,176 --> 00:05:06,136
{\an8}it's like a drug.
I mean, you just can't stop.

98
00:05:06,637 --> 00:05:08,055
{\an8}Yeah, sounds exciting.

99
00:05:08,680 --> 00:05:11,683
{\an8}So can I just show up and start panning
or digging or what have you

100
00:05:11,809 --> 00:05:13,560
{\an8}or do I need a permit
or a license or something?

101
00:05:14,269 --> 00:05:16,563
{\an8}- Check.
- No. All you gotta do to stake a claim

102
00:05:16,730 --> 00:05:19,191
{\an8}is find open public land,
and then you gotta mark it

103
00:05:19,358 --> 00:05:21,735
{\an8}and register with the government
and stuff. Five.

104
00:05:22,820 --> 00:05:23,862
{\an8}I'll call that.

105
00:05:23,987 --> 00:05:26,240
{\an8}Wes Attwood will sell you one that's already
registered, too if you want.

106
00:05:26,365 --> 00:05:27,574
{\an8}It saves you time.

107
00:05:27,950 --> 00:05:28,992
{\an8}Raise you 10.

108
00:05:30,285 --> 00:05:32,830
{\an8}Okay, well, uh, I'm gonna see your 15

109
00:05:34,081 --> 00:05:36,792
and I'm gonna raise you
the same.

110
00:05:38,168 --> 00:05:39,378
{\an8}Sheriff says there's nothing up there.

111
00:05:39,503 --> 00:05:41,547
{\an8}He says that you guys
are just kidding yourselves.

112
00:05:42,130 --> 00:05:43,757
Yeah, he would say that.

113
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:46,760
{\an8}- He would?
- All these townspeople

114
00:05:46,885 --> 00:05:48,637
{\an8}own claims up in these hills themselves
so...

115
00:05:48,804 --> 00:05:51,390
Exactly, and they wanna
keep it all for themselves.

116
00:05:51,765 --> 00:05:52,808
I'm gonna call you.

117
00:05:53,308 --> 00:05:54,434
Stands to reason.

118
00:05:56,562 --> 00:05:58,564
Oh... that's my ride.

119
00:05:59,147 --> 00:06:00,440
Well, folks, uh...

120
00:06:01,191 --> 00:06:02,442
- It's been fun, but...
- What?

121
00:06:02,609 --> 00:06:03,652
I have to go.

122
00:06:03,944 --> 00:06:05,404
Well, come on. You can't just leave.

123
00:06:05,946 --> 00:06:07,197
We barely took any of your money.

124
00:06:07,614 --> 00:06:11,243
Two pair, busted flush, pair of Jacks.

125
00:06:13,787 --> 00:06:14,997
Don't let us keep you.

126
00:06:16,248 --> 00:06:17,291
Hey, Lisbon.

127
00:06:18,375 --> 00:06:20,252
But no. Hightower.

128
00:06:22,337 --> 00:06:23,672
I didn't even know you had legs.

129
00:06:24,381 --> 00:06:25,424
Let's go.

130
00:06:26,133 --> 00:06:28,218
- What were you doing back there?
- Five card draw.

131
00:06:28,802 --> 00:06:30,929
- For money?
- No. For gold.

132
00:06:31,680 --> 00:06:34,641
Oh, gold. Gold.
That makes it all right, then.

133
00:06:35,517 --> 00:06:36,560
And gossip.

134
00:06:36,935 --> 00:06:39,396
Victim Rick Loomis was
a former software developer.

135
00:06:39,688 --> 00:06:41,315
Came up here after he got laid off.

136
00:06:41,440 --> 00:06:43,609
His girlfriend is Cheryl Meade.

137
00:06:44,026 --> 00:06:45,819
They've been together since his divorce.

138
00:06:46,236 --> 00:06:48,655
Rick enjoyed working on his truck
with his son Jeff

139
00:06:48,780 --> 00:06:51,825
age 17 and smart, but a little hostile.

140
00:06:53,452 --> 00:06:55,037
You seem a little keyed up, Madeleine.

141
00:06:56,580 --> 00:06:57,623
Something wrong?

142
00:06:58,582 --> 00:06:59,625
Something personal?

143
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:01,084
None of your business.

144
00:07:03,211 --> 00:07:04,254
Okay.

145
00:07:04,379 --> 00:07:07,925
We're sorry for your loss, miss Meade,
Jeff, but we need to know

146
00:07:08,050 --> 00:07:10,761
why you didn't file
a missing person's report for Rick.

147
00:07:11,011 --> 00:07:14,139
Well, Rick had been, uh,
mining off the grid recently

148
00:07:14,264 --> 00:07:17,142
away from his regular
claim near the creek.

149
00:07:17,851 --> 00:07:19,811
Sometimes my dad
would be gone for a few days

150
00:07:19,937 --> 00:07:22,189
and he'd never call to let us know.

151
00:07:22,981 --> 00:07:24,024
Good school.

152
00:07:24,232 --> 00:07:28,195
How's it been going...
your dad's gold claim?

153
00:07:29,029 --> 00:07:31,406
- Great at first...
- Then nothing but mud.

154
00:07:33,408 --> 00:07:35,953
It would've come good eventually.
He would've hit pay dirt.

155
00:07:36,536 --> 00:07:38,997
- Wes Attwood sell him that claim?
- Yeah. Mr. Attwood.

156
00:07:39,498 --> 00:07:41,750
Did, um, Rick carry a gun
with him

157
00:07:41,875 --> 00:07:43,710
when he would go off by himself?

158
00:07:43,835 --> 00:07:45,587
No. Rick didn't believe in guns.

159
00:07:45,712 --> 00:07:48,298
- He didn't think guns exist?
- No, he didn't like them.

160
00:07:48,423 --> 00:07:50,133
Me neither. Terribly dangerous.

161
00:07:50,300 --> 00:07:52,219
Have you seen the statistics
on police shootings?

162
00:07:52,427 --> 00:07:55,180
One bullet out of every ten
hits a bad guy.

163
00:07:55,722 --> 00:07:57,057
And that's the trained people.

164
00:07:57,557 --> 00:07:58,600
No offense.

165
00:07:59,351 --> 00:08:01,103
This is not useful. Can I go now?

166
00:08:03,313 --> 00:08:04,356
Yeah, sure.

167
00:08:10,821 --> 00:08:12,239
It was Rick's idea
to come out here, wasn't it?

168
00:08:12,406 --> 00:08:13,532
To follow the gold dream.

169
00:08:14,533 --> 00:08:16,702
When Rick had a passion,
there was no stopping him.

170
00:08:17,369 --> 00:08:18,829
I loved that about him.

171
00:08:19,371 --> 00:08:21,748
But you had to give up
a successful career of your own.

172
00:08:22,624 --> 00:08:25,085
High-end sales type of business.

173
00:08:26,086 --> 00:08:28,463
Following someone else's dream
must be, uh...

174
00:08:28,964 --> 00:08:30,424
At times, a little frustrating.

175
00:08:30,590 --> 00:08:32,217
We all made sacrifices.

176
00:08:32,342 --> 00:08:35,220
Jeff put off going to college.

177
00:08:35,387 --> 00:08:37,597
Oh. Rick raided the college fund, did he?

178
00:08:38,015 --> 00:08:41,560
Yeah. We're broke.
Okay, we're flat broke. But...

179
00:08:44,229 --> 00:08:45,272
We had each other.

180
00:08:48,984 --> 00:08:50,027
Cheryl, look at me.

181
00:08:51,820 --> 00:08:55,532
There's no need to pretend
and I mean that in the nicest possible way

182
00:08:56,158 --> 00:08:57,868
but there's a whole lot of guilt

183
00:08:58,952 --> 00:09:00,328
in there with that grief

184
00:09:00,704 --> 00:09:03,999
and just a smidgen,
just a smidgen, of relief.

185
00:09:05,125 --> 00:09:07,294
Right? Were you cheating on Loomis?

186
00:09:07,461 --> 00:09:08,503
- No!
- Patrick!

187
00:09:08,628 --> 00:09:10,130
- No!
- Okay, just a thought.

188
00:09:10,756 --> 00:09:11,923
You're guilty about something.

189
00:09:12,049 --> 00:09:14,051
Where were you on Monday night
when he was murdered?

190
00:09:14,634 --> 00:09:17,179
I, I was at the diner, waitressing.

191
00:09:17,804 --> 00:09:18,972
I can't believe that you think
that I would hurt him.

192
00:09:19,097 --> 00:09:20,932
All right. Okay.
I'll be leaving right now.

193
00:09:22,100 --> 00:09:23,143
Sorry.

194
00:09:25,353 --> 00:09:27,647
There was no reason
to treat her disrespectfully.

195
00:09:28,273 --> 00:09:29,691
I was probing her.

196
00:09:29,858 --> 00:09:32,402
A certain amount of disrespect
is necessary. It's, uh...

197
00:09:32,736 --> 00:09:34,654
It's like getting frisked at the airport.

198
00:09:37,199 --> 00:09:39,201
Probing. All right.

199
00:09:40,368 --> 00:09:42,746
Okay. Well, she did seem like
she was hiding something.

200
00:09:43,705 --> 00:09:45,332
I'll get the team
to look into her financials

201
00:09:45,457 --> 00:09:46,750
while we talk to sheriff Hughes.

202
00:09:47,167 --> 00:09:48,210
Well, that sounds fun.

203
00:09:48,335 --> 00:09:50,295
I bet there's a good diner in town
where you can drop me off.

204
00:09:50,587 --> 00:09:52,214
I bet there is. Why don't I do that?

205
00:09:52,506 --> 00:09:54,966
Tell a lot about a place
by the quality of the eggs.

206
00:10:00,847 --> 00:10:02,057
Checked into the bank records.

207
00:10:02,182 --> 00:10:05,644
Victim had an account with his girlfriend,
all of 88 dollars and 32 cents.

208
00:10:05,894 --> 00:10:08,105
But here's the thing. Cheryl Meade,
she had a separate account

209
00:10:08,230 --> 00:10:09,856
her name only, over 5 grand.

210
00:10:10,148 --> 00:10:12,943
The girlfriend? She told Hightower
she was flat broke.

211
00:10:13,318 --> 00:10:14,361
She lied.

212
00:10:14,486 --> 00:10:15,529
Cheryl Meade also bought
a one-way ticket

213
00:10:15,654 --> 00:10:16,696
to San Francisco a week ago.

214
00:10:16,822 --> 00:10:17,864
Well, maybe she wanted to put
some distance

215
00:10:17,989 --> 00:10:19,032
between herself and a dead boyfriend.

216
00:10:19,157 --> 00:10:20,408
Rick Loomis recently bought a gun.

217
00:10:20,534 --> 00:10:22,536
Colt .45, same make
as the murder weapon.

218
00:10:22,702 --> 00:10:24,454
The girlfriend told Hightower
Loomis didn't own one.

219
00:10:24,621 --> 00:10:26,790
- So either she knew about it or...
- Or she's lying about that, too.

220
00:10:27,249 --> 00:10:28,625
- Well, let's bring her in here.
- Don't worry, boss.

221
00:10:28,750 --> 00:10:29,793
We already got it covered.

222
00:10:30,168 --> 00:10:32,587
Okay. I'll just stay here and, um...

223
00:10:33,839 --> 00:10:34,881
Not do anything.

224
00:10:36,174 --> 00:10:37,217
"Hello?"

225
00:10:38,552 --> 00:10:40,971
Mr. Attwood? Anyone home?

226
00:10:41,263 --> 00:10:43,056
I'm Wes Attwood. How you doing?

227
00:10:43,723 --> 00:10:46,143
- You're the police, right?
- Well, more of a hobbyist.

228
00:10:46,476 --> 00:10:48,687
Patrick Jane. Your assistant told me
where to find you.

229
00:10:48,812 --> 00:10:50,856
Hey, I'm Dean Puttock.
Nice to meet you.

230
00:10:51,773 --> 00:10:52,899
After I sell you a claim

231
00:10:53,108 --> 00:10:55,193
Dean will sell you anything
you need to find gold.

232
00:10:55,360 --> 00:10:58,947
Sluices, high-bankers, pans...
he's the go-to guy.

233
00:10:59,322 --> 00:11:02,492
That's right. We got a 10% off sale
on pumps all week long, so...

234
00:11:02,617 --> 00:11:03,869
- Good to know.
- Yeah.

235
00:11:03,994 --> 00:11:06,204
I gotta get back to the shop. You guys
need anything, let me know.

236
00:11:06,329 --> 00:11:07,747
- Okay.
- Good seeing you. Nice to meet you.

237
00:11:07,873 --> 00:11:09,207
Nice to meet you, too, Dean.

238
00:11:09,374 --> 00:11:12,752
Hey, you folks mind if I join you?
Just to get a feel for the place?

239
00:11:13,003 --> 00:11:16,798
Uh... I don't know.
Wes, we're the customers, right?

240
00:11:17,716 --> 00:11:20,427
Sure thing, Sonya, but...
the more, the merrier, Mr. Jane.

241
00:11:21,303 --> 00:11:23,847
Just showing Jack and Sonya here
a claim I have for sale.

242
00:11:24,890 --> 00:11:26,683
- Watch and learn.
- All right.

243
00:11:29,352 --> 00:11:30,395
All right.

244
00:11:31,563 --> 00:11:34,774
You knew Rick Loomis, didn't you?
The, uh, miner that died in the park?

245
00:11:35,525 --> 00:11:38,361
Yeah. Yeah, I sold Loomis a claim
when he first got to Hadley.

246
00:11:39,029 --> 00:11:41,573
- Real tragedy. Nice guy.
- Ah.

247
00:11:41,865 --> 00:11:45,118
So, it's the bends in the river
where you're gonna find the gold, you see?

248
00:11:45,243 --> 00:11:46,286
- Uh-huh.
- Okay.

249
00:11:46,536 --> 00:11:48,872
It's heavier, so it falls into the silt.

250
00:11:49,497 --> 00:11:51,458
You just gotta wash the silt away...

251
00:11:58,924 --> 00:11:59,966
What do you see there?

252
00:12:00,884 --> 00:12:03,720
Oh, my God. Jack, gold.

253
00:12:03,845 --> 00:12:04,930
- Oh, my God.
- Oh, my...

254
00:12:05,639 --> 00:12:06,681
I wonder what it's worth.

255
00:12:07,140 --> 00:12:10,393
Did you know only 10% of the gold
in the Sierra Nevadas

256
00:12:10,518 --> 00:12:12,062
was found during the original gold rush?

257
00:12:12,812 --> 00:12:14,856
Billions of dollars are out there...

258
00:12:16,233 --> 00:12:17,400
Just waiting to be discovered.

259
00:12:18,944 --> 00:12:19,986
Can I see?

260
00:12:20,153 --> 00:12:21,863
Um... yeah.

261
00:12:22,948 --> 00:12:23,990
Wow.

262
00:12:24,658 --> 00:12:25,700
- Yeah.
- It's cool.

263
00:12:26,117 --> 00:12:27,869
Yeah. Hey, Wes...

264
00:12:29,454 --> 00:12:30,538
Wanna try just here?

265
00:12:32,749 --> 00:12:34,417
How about we let the new owners
pan over there?

266
00:12:36,920 --> 00:12:37,963
Well, I'll take it.

267
00:12:38,088 --> 00:12:40,840
- Hey, hey. We... We get first dibs.
- Hang on, pal.

268
00:12:41,007 --> 00:12:42,592
Dibs? What are we? Children?

269
00:12:43,343 --> 00:12:44,678
I'm just kidding. It's all yours.

270
00:12:45,011 --> 00:12:46,721
Do you have another claim
you can sell me?

271
00:12:50,684 --> 00:12:52,727
- Yeah, I-I do have other claims.
- Yeah?

272
00:12:53,186 --> 00:12:54,479
Great spots, but, uh...

273
00:12:55,355 --> 00:12:57,649
- Aren't you here on police business?
- Yeah. So?

274
00:12:58,024 --> 00:12:59,943
It doesn't mean I can't mine for gold.

275
00:13:00,485 --> 00:13:03,363
- Do whatever I want.
- Okay. Sure thing.

276
00:13:03,947 --> 00:13:04,990
Cool.

277
00:13:05,699 --> 00:13:07,742
I got another super-hot site.

278
00:13:08,493 --> 00:13:12,414
Black sand and quartz all up
and down the bed stream.

279
00:13:12,831 --> 00:13:14,749
Black sand and quartz...
that's the good stuff, isn't it?

280
00:13:14,874 --> 00:13:16,209
- Yup. This is the site for you.
- Oh, yeah.

281
00:13:16,334 --> 00:13:18,378
It's up the creek there
about half a mile.

282
00:13:18,545 --> 00:13:20,213
- And where are we here?
- About here.

283
00:13:20,338 --> 00:13:22,173
Okay. So can we go
take a look at it now?

284
00:13:23,550 --> 00:13:25,969
You know, I'm, I'm kinda jammed
today, but, uh...

285
00:13:26,469 --> 00:13:27,721
What do you say
I meet you in the parking lot

286
00:13:27,846 --> 00:13:30,515
bright and early tomorrow morning?
Say, 8:00 A.M.?

287
00:13:30,682 --> 00:13:32,350
Tomorrow at 8:00 A.M.
in the parking lot.

288
00:13:32,475 --> 00:13:33,852
- Look forward to it.
- Good.

289
00:13:34,561 --> 00:13:35,937
Yeah, well, good luck to you.

290
00:13:36,563 --> 00:13:38,773
Oh, I won't need luck.
I'm feeling the gold.

291
00:13:38,982 --> 00:13:40,066
I feel rich already.

292
00:13:41,151 --> 00:13:42,193
- You take it easy.
- Yeah.

293
00:13:42,319 --> 00:13:44,946
- That's the spirit. Good.
- I'll see you later. 8:00 A.M.

294
00:13:45,071 --> 00:13:46,114
All right.

295
00:13:50,577 --> 00:13:52,329
Rick owns a colt .45.
Where is it?

296
00:13:52,662 --> 00:13:53,705
A gun?

297
00:13:54,622 --> 00:13:55,665
Rick?

298
00:13:57,042 --> 00:13:58,543
What would Rick need a gun for?

299
00:13:59,336 --> 00:14:01,463
You also told us you were broke,
but you have $5,000.

300
00:14:01,588 --> 00:14:02,630
That's not broke.

301
00:14:04,924 --> 00:14:06,718
That's my money, and you can't
touch it.

302
00:14:06,885 --> 00:14:08,678
We don't plan on touching it.
Where'd you get it?

303
00:14:09,054 --> 00:14:10,096
Waitressing.

304
00:14:12,432 --> 00:14:13,892
$5,000 tucked away.

305
00:14:16,311 --> 00:14:19,397
And you booked a seat on flight 465
to San Fran for this morning.

306
00:14:20,231 --> 00:14:21,274
No. I mean...

307
00:14:22,192 --> 00:14:23,234
I mean, ye...

308
00:14:24,819 --> 00:14:26,112
I booked that a week ago.

309
00:14:27,572 --> 00:14:30,367
- Before he died.
- Exactly. It looks like a plan.

310
00:14:30,533 --> 00:14:33,286
- Some plan. I didn't go, did I?
- Why not?

311
00:14:33,912 --> 00:14:35,413
Because I knew it would look bad.

312
00:14:35,747 --> 00:14:36,915
Why were you planning on going?

313
00:14:38,833 --> 00:14:41,211
'Cause I was... leaving Rick.

314
00:14:41,336 --> 00:14:42,796
I couldn't take it here anymore.

315
00:14:44,798 --> 00:14:46,299
- Did Rick know that?
- No.

316
00:14:46,758 --> 00:14:49,177
Rick was blind to everything
but the gold.

317
00:14:49,302 --> 00:14:51,429
He's been like that
ever since we got here.

318
00:14:51,554 --> 00:14:53,348
He, he would find a little nugget

319
00:14:53,515 --> 00:14:55,683
and he would get all excited
and come running in

320
00:14:55,809 --> 00:14:57,894
saying,
"We're rich. I hit the mother lode."

321
00:14:58,436 --> 00:14:59,646
The first three times, I believed him

322
00:14:59,771 --> 00:15:02,690
and then after the 20th time,
I couldn't take it anymore.

323
00:15:04,859 --> 00:15:07,362
Look, I loved him. I did.

324
00:15:09,114 --> 00:15:10,865
But I fell in love
with a regular guy

325
00:15:10,990 --> 00:15:13,910
with an apartment
and a job and clean clothes

326
00:15:14,244 --> 00:15:17,580
not some obsessive
scratching in the dirt.

327
00:15:19,374 --> 00:15:22,419
But I need you to know, I'm...
I'm not a bad person.

328
00:15:24,254 --> 00:15:25,839
I don't make that kind of evaluation.

329
00:15:40,603 --> 00:15:42,021
Uh, excuse me, sir.

330
00:15:42,522 --> 00:15:44,315
Do you know which store
is Wes Attwood's?

331
00:15:44,858 --> 00:15:45,900
Yep.

332
00:15:48,445 --> 00:15:49,529
You wanna tell me?

333
00:15:50,738 --> 00:15:51,781
Right there.

334
00:15:53,116 --> 00:15:54,159
Thank you.

335
00:15:54,659 --> 00:15:56,161
'Course, it's not open right now.

336
00:15:58,163 --> 00:15:59,205
Of course.

337
00:16:00,707 --> 00:16:03,626
- Do you know how I can contact him?
- Yep.

338
00:16:06,296 --> 00:16:10,008
Uh, you don't wanna get her mad.
She's, uh, stressed enough already.

339
00:16:13,052 --> 00:16:15,180
- Where have you been?
- In the diner.

340
00:16:15,388 --> 00:16:17,015
Not when I went by there
several hours ago.

341
00:16:17,182 --> 00:16:18,224
Good eggs.

342
00:16:18,475 --> 00:16:20,602
Two loosely scrambled on toast
is the test

343
00:16:20,727 --> 00:16:22,687
and these were just so.

344
00:16:23,229 --> 00:16:24,939
Then I took a nice walk
in the countryside.

345
00:16:25,106 --> 00:16:26,900
You know, there's no cell phone range
in these hills

346
00:16:27,066 --> 00:16:28,401
which I find very refreshing.

347
00:16:30,361 --> 00:16:32,447
I am going to go see Wes Attwood.

348
00:16:32,864 --> 00:16:33,907
Perhaps you'll join me?

349
00:16:34,073 --> 00:16:37,076
You're really committed to keeping
your temper contained, huh?

350
00:16:37,827 --> 00:16:38,870
What's up with that?

351
00:16:38,995 --> 00:16:40,872
You're really committed
to testing my temper, huh?

352
00:16:41,122 --> 00:16:43,124
- What's up with that?
- Idle curiosity.

353
00:16:46,377 --> 00:16:48,630
- What, what are you doing?
- I'm hiding from Wes Attwood.

354
00:16:49,130 --> 00:16:51,508
- Why?
- Because we're gonna follow him.

355
00:17:00,642 --> 00:17:02,101
- Okay.
- Okay.

356
00:17:13,071 --> 00:17:15,323
- What's he doing?
- He's trying to defraud me.

357
00:17:17,742 --> 00:17:19,619
His shotgun is filled with gold flakes.

358
00:17:19,744 --> 00:17:22,705
He's salting the claims he sells
to make 'em appear richer.

359
00:17:24,165 --> 00:17:26,292
Hey, Wes!
Looks like we're both a little early.

360
00:17:26,417 --> 00:17:27,835
Whoa, whoa... easy.

361
00:17:28,336 --> 00:17:29,879
- Put the gun down!
- Okay, okay.

362
00:17:30,004 --> 00:17:31,839
- Put the shotgun down!
- Chill. Don't, don't shoot.

363
00:17:31,965 --> 00:17:33,800
- I'm not resisting.
- Turn around.

364
00:17:34,008 --> 00:17:35,051
- I'm not resisting...
- Turn around.

365
00:17:35,176 --> 00:17:36,219
Okay, okay.

366
00:17:38,012 --> 00:17:39,347
Look, this is a misunderst...

367
00:17:39,973 --> 00:17:42,934
You... you can't cuff me.
I haven't committed a crime.

368
00:17:43,601 --> 00:17:45,895
Try pointing a gun at a police officer,
for starters. Move.

369
00:17:46,020 --> 00:17:48,439
- This is outrageous.
- That's the spirit.

370
00:18:02,577 --> 00:18:03,828
Gold in a shotgun cartridge.

371
00:18:04,912 --> 00:18:05,955
Clever.

372
00:18:06,289 --> 00:18:07,748
Mr. Attwood, let me lay it out for you.

373
00:18:08,791 --> 00:18:11,127
Looks to us like Rick Loomis
caught you salting the gold claims.

374
00:18:11,502 --> 00:18:12,712
"He would've been plenty mad."

375
00:18:13,921 --> 00:18:16,007
"You would've had
to kill him to keep him quiet."

376
00:18:16,507 --> 00:18:19,176
"Hey, now, I didn't kill anybody."

377
00:18:21,012 --> 00:18:22,722
I mean, I'm a killer salesman, but...

378
00:18:23,347 --> 00:18:24,473
You know, I'm not a killer.

379
00:18:25,182 --> 00:18:27,810
Ask yourself whether this is the right
moment for humor, Mr. Attwood.

380
00:18:34,191 --> 00:18:35,651
I am a seller of hope.

381
00:18:36,902 --> 00:18:40,364
I was simply helping folks
visualize the future.

382
00:18:41,824 --> 00:18:43,200
"Great marketing is not a crime."

383
00:18:44,201 --> 00:18:46,120
"And I wouldn't murder anyone
to cover it up."

384
00:18:46,454 --> 00:18:49,540
We'll see. We can hold you
on the fraud charges

385
00:18:50,041 --> 00:18:51,375
and see about the murder as we go.

386
00:18:52,126 --> 00:18:53,169
Oh, come on.

387
00:18:53,502 --> 00:18:55,671
I can have a lawyer here in half an hour.
Be reasonable.

388
00:18:56,631 --> 00:18:58,924
There's plenty of people
that could've killed that guy.

389
00:19:00,384 --> 00:19:03,054
Fraud alone can get you ten years,
depending on the judge.

390
00:19:04,972 --> 00:19:07,850
Have you even checked into the
sneak thief that's been targeting miners?

391
00:19:08,809 --> 00:19:09,894
"Stealing their equipment?"

392
00:19:11,228 --> 00:19:12,605
Loomis got hit ten days ago.

393
00:19:12,730 --> 00:19:15,900
Bastard cleaned him out.
That's who killed him, I promise you.

394
00:19:16,400 --> 00:19:19,320
If that's true, I wonder why
Sheriff Hughes didn't tell us about it.

395
00:19:19,445 --> 00:19:21,238
That's why I hate
these small town investigations.

396
00:19:21,447 --> 00:19:22,782
You never know if the guys
have done the math.

397
00:19:22,907 --> 00:19:25,493
I'm gonna follow up with Hughes.
Ow.

398
00:19:26,369 --> 00:19:28,412
- You good?
- Screw it. You go.

399
00:19:28,537 --> 00:19:29,580
Yeah.

400
00:19:30,956 --> 00:19:33,167
Why rob Loomis and
then kill him ten days later?

401
00:19:34,251 --> 00:19:36,587
Maybe Loomis found out
who it was that stole from him

402
00:19:36,712 --> 00:19:37,755
and confronted him.

403
00:19:38,964 --> 00:19:41,258
Yeah. That must be what happened.

404
00:19:45,888 --> 00:19:46,972
Something you care to share?

405
00:19:47,765 --> 00:19:51,018
No. Sorry, just a funny thought, is all.

406
00:19:52,853 --> 00:19:54,897
Can I go now,
or are you gonna charge me?

407
00:19:55,690 --> 00:19:56,732
I'll let you know.

408
00:19:58,609 --> 00:19:59,694
All right. That's odd.

409
00:20:00,695 --> 00:20:02,655
Okay. Well, charge Wes Attwood
with fraud

410
00:20:02,780 --> 00:20:06,325
if he can post bail, let him go
and put a name to this local thief.

411
00:20:07,618 --> 00:20:09,954
All right.
Okay, so sheriff Hughes confirms

412
00:20:10,079 --> 00:20:12,123
that there has been
a string of equipment thefts.

413
00:20:12,248 --> 00:20:16,001
But he says that Rick Loomis
never filed a police report.

414
00:20:16,127 --> 00:20:19,630
Hmm. Why not, we wonder?
Let's go talk sluice pumps.

415
00:20:22,717 --> 00:20:25,136
- So you play bad cop.
- I'll be me, thank you.

416
00:20:25,344 --> 00:20:28,013
- Right. Exactly what I said.
- Funny.

417
00:20:29,724 --> 00:20:30,933
So funny.

418
00:20:36,897 --> 00:20:37,940
I hit a big one.

419
00:20:38,357 --> 00:20:39,692
Wow. They're huge.

420
00:20:39,817 --> 00:20:41,902
- You cashing in?
- Hell, no!

421
00:20:42,194 --> 00:20:43,863
Just when I hit good dirt? Nah.

422
00:20:44,071 --> 00:20:45,990
I'm buying a better pump
and foot valves.

423
00:20:46,157 --> 00:20:47,366
Hey, Mr. Jane. How ya doin'?

424
00:20:47,658 --> 00:20:48,993
- Good. How are you?
- Good.

425
00:20:49,118 --> 00:20:51,162
Oh, this is, uh, Special Agent Hightower.

426
00:20:51,328 --> 00:20:54,248
We believe Rick Loomis bought some,
uh, new mining equipment from you

427
00:20:54,373 --> 00:20:55,958
after his old equipment
had been stolen.

428
00:20:56,208 --> 00:20:59,253
Yes, he did. He was in here,
I'd say, about nine days ago.

429
00:20:59,378 --> 00:21:00,421
What did he buy?

430
00:21:00,796 --> 00:21:02,590
Whole bunch of stuff.
He spent over 10 grand.

431
00:21:03,215 --> 00:21:04,467
Maybe we could see an invoice?

432
00:21:05,134 --> 00:21:06,552
Yeah. Give me a second.
I'll pull it up.

433
00:21:07,553 --> 00:21:11,932
Um, would you mind terribly
if, if I held one of your, uh...

434
00:21:12,308 --> 00:21:13,642
your nuggets in my hand?

435
00:21:13,976 --> 00:21:17,104
- Mm... sure, I guess.
- Well...

436
00:21:17,605 --> 00:21:18,647
Thank you.

437
00:21:18,981 --> 00:21:20,024
Thanks.

438
00:21:22,026 --> 00:21:23,986
A g-force rock crusher.

439
00:21:24,445 --> 00:21:28,699
And a series 54 Pulverizer?
Man. I wish I had one of those.

440
00:21:29,617 --> 00:21:31,619
That's the, that's the best
for hard rock mining.

441
00:21:31,869 --> 00:21:33,579
Yep. That's some good gear
for sure.

442
00:21:33,871 --> 00:21:35,164
Hard rock mining, is that in the creek?

443
00:21:35,581 --> 00:21:36,791
No, no. Creek panning's
a whole other thing.

444
00:21:36,916 --> 00:21:39,794
Hard rock, it's like it sounds.
Uh, dry mining up in the hills.

445
00:21:40,002 --> 00:21:42,713
Did he mention to you that his mining
equipment had been stolen?

446
00:21:43,631 --> 00:21:46,050
He didn't. He said that he got
sick and tired of panning.

447
00:21:46,175 --> 00:21:47,301
Wanted to try something new.

448
00:21:47,551 --> 00:21:50,471
Seemed a little bit like a hail Mary
desperation kinda play to me.

449
00:21:50,596 --> 00:21:52,348
But hey, chasing his dream, you know?

450
00:21:52,473 --> 00:21:54,058
He never did have any patience.

451
00:21:54,850 --> 00:21:56,769
You know, God rest his soul.

452
00:21:57,728 --> 00:21:59,647
Right. Thank you.

453
00:22:01,649 --> 00:22:02,691
Hey!

454
00:22:06,278 --> 00:22:08,405
You got me. Worth a try.

455
00:22:10,157 --> 00:22:11,200
Sorry, ma'am.

456
00:22:11,951 --> 00:22:13,744
- Hello?
- Ma'am? Rigsby.

457
00:22:13,953 --> 00:22:15,287
So, we may have found our thief.

458
00:22:15,621 --> 00:22:17,706
Scott Munroe.
He's a townie with a long rap sheet.

459
00:22:17,832 --> 00:22:20,376
Assault with a deadly weapon,
B&E, and, wait for it...

460
00:22:20,709 --> 00:22:22,169
stealing mining equipment.

461
00:22:22,545 --> 00:22:23,963
"Got outta prison six months ago."

462
00:22:24,463 --> 00:22:26,632
Good work. Scott Munroe.
You got an address?

463
00:22:26,924 --> 00:22:28,092
Yeah, I'm gonna send it to you...

464
00:22:30,094 --> 00:22:31,428
"Rigsby? An address?"

465
00:22:31,929 --> 00:22:34,473
Uh, y-yes, ma'am.
Your kids are here?

466
00:22:36,475 --> 00:22:37,518
What?

467
00:22:40,396 --> 00:22:43,774
Ma'am? Your, uh, husband
had to go somewhere, I guess.

468
00:22:44,191 --> 00:22:46,986
He left Mimi and Will
with Jean at the front desk.

469
00:22:48,946 --> 00:22:50,573
Damn. Damn. Damn.

470
00:22:53,742 --> 00:22:54,785
Lisbon here.

471
00:22:54,910 --> 00:22:58,163
Listen, don't worry.
We can look after them until you get back.

472
00:22:58,414 --> 00:22:59,456
Lisbon, thank you.

473
00:23:00,374 --> 00:23:01,792
I appreciate it, truly.

474
00:23:03,002 --> 00:23:04,044
I, um...

475
00:23:06,130 --> 00:23:07,172
Thank you.

476
00:23:08,841 --> 00:23:10,301
May I have a word with Mimi, please?

477
00:23:10,759 --> 00:23:12,428
All right. It's your mom.

478
00:23:14,555 --> 00:23:15,598
Hi, mommy.

479
00:23:17,933 --> 00:23:21,020
- Hi, baby. You okay?
- "Uh-huh."

480
00:23:21,437 --> 00:23:22,938
Look, I'm gonna be back
in a few hours

481
00:23:23,105 --> 00:23:25,024
but I want you to, uh, be a good girl

482
00:23:25,190 --> 00:23:27,151
and I want you to listen
to what Ms. Lisbon says

483
00:23:27,276 --> 00:23:29,069
and I want you to look after
your brother, okay?

484
00:23:29,570 --> 00:23:31,780
- Okay.
- Mimi, I love you.

485
00:23:33,657 --> 00:23:35,492
- Bye.
- Bye.

486
00:23:41,040 --> 00:23:42,207
So, uh...

487
00:23:43,417 --> 00:23:44,668
Who's Scott Munroe?

488
00:23:47,796 --> 00:23:49,465
Local mining equipment thief.

489
00:23:50,174 --> 00:23:52,384
Excellent.
Let's go talk to him, shall we?

490
00:23:52,718 --> 00:23:54,136
Thanks for not asking questions.

491
00:23:54,261 --> 00:23:57,056
Oh, no need to ask questions
when you know all the answers.

492
00:23:59,683 --> 00:24:00,726
So...

493
00:24:02,269 --> 00:24:03,646
- Cookies?
- Okay.

494
00:24:08,984 --> 00:24:11,737
- Are you disabled?
- No. No, I'm injured.

495
00:24:12,071 --> 00:24:14,698
Not that disabled is bad, but I'm not.
I'm injured.

496
00:24:15,783 --> 00:24:17,159
You walk like disabled.

497
00:24:27,670 --> 00:24:29,421
So, no questions, but, uh...

498
00:24:31,548 --> 00:24:32,883
we can talk if you want.

499
00:24:33,217 --> 00:24:34,301
Talk about what?

500
00:24:35,511 --> 00:24:38,055
- I'll take that as a no.
- Yes. No.

501
00:24:38,722 --> 00:24:39,765
Scott?

502
00:24:40,349 --> 00:24:41,558
Scott Munroe?

503
00:24:42,977 --> 00:24:44,144
Scott Munroe?

504
00:24:48,899 --> 00:24:49,942
CBI.

505
00:25:00,160 --> 00:25:01,370
Oh, hell...

506
00:25:01,996 --> 00:25:03,664
Is it Scott Munroe?

507
00:25:05,499 --> 00:25:09,294
No. Wes Attwood.
Gold claims dealer.

508
00:25:10,337 --> 00:25:11,380
That's odd.

509
00:25:12,589 --> 00:25:15,342
- Odd?
- It's sad, too, but I'm...

510
00:25:16,468 --> 00:25:17,636
I'm just saying it's odd...

511
00:25:18,721 --> 00:25:20,222
that it's not Scott Munroe.

512
00:25:26,059 --> 00:25:28,937
- Where's Mimi and will?
- Tearing up paper in my office.

513
00:25:29,229 --> 00:25:31,148
- What's going on with Munroe?
- Well, we've got a BOLO out

514
00:25:31,273 --> 00:25:33,275
and Sheriff Hughes
is putting up roadblocks.

515
00:25:33,567 --> 00:25:34,901
Hadley's pretty much on lockdown.

516
00:25:35,193 --> 00:25:36,695
Why would Munroe kill Attwood?

517
00:25:36,862 --> 00:25:38,572
Well, maybe he was conducting
an investigation of his own.

518
00:25:38,780 --> 00:25:40,615
You know, trying to figure out
if Munroe killed Loomis.

519
00:25:41,324 --> 00:25:42,367
Found out the hard way.

520
00:25:43,660 --> 00:25:44,703
Rigsby.

521
00:25:45,495 --> 00:25:47,581
No, don't move in. You'll scare him off.
We're leaving now.

522
00:25:48,498 --> 00:25:50,625
Munroe's been spotted in a local bar
that his cousin works at.

523
00:25:50,751 --> 00:25:52,002
Go. Both of you.

524
00:25:57,924 --> 00:25:58,967
Hey, boss.

525
00:25:59,384 --> 00:26:00,427
No, they're fine.

526
00:26:01,470 --> 00:26:02,804
Y-yeah. Uh-huh.

527
00:26:05,182 --> 00:26:06,224
Uh-huh.

528
00:26:06,683 --> 00:26:08,977
All right.
All right. Hold on a second.

529
00:26:09,519 --> 00:26:13,148
Whoa! Look at this!
Excellent work, guys.

530
00:26:13,356 --> 00:26:17,152
Now, we are gonna take this paper
and put it in these bags.

531
00:26:17,277 --> 00:26:18,487
But first, say hi to mommy.

532
00:26:20,489 --> 00:26:21,531
- Hi, Mom.
- Hi, Mom.

533
00:26:21,656 --> 00:26:24,868
- Hey, mommy, guess what?
- We're tearing up paper.

534
00:26:25,118 --> 00:26:26,328
Tearing up paper?

535
00:26:27,579 --> 00:26:28,622
Okay.

536
00:26:28,747 --> 00:26:32,084
All right.
Well, Mimi, eat all your snack, okay?

537
00:26:33,126 --> 00:26:34,544
All right. I love you.

538
00:26:35,837 --> 00:26:38,173
Give the phone back to,
um, Agent Lisbon for me.

539
00:26:39,508 --> 00:26:40,550
Okay.

540
00:26:41,343 --> 00:26:43,637
Hey. You're sending Rigsby
and Van Pelt?

541
00:26:44,638 --> 00:26:46,681
Okay, great.
Keep me apprised on Monroe.

542
00:26:47,849 --> 00:26:49,434
All right. Thanks. Bye.

543
00:26:51,228 --> 00:26:52,395
Chamomile tea...

544
00:26:52,938 --> 00:26:54,397
excellent for repressed anger.

545
00:26:56,817 --> 00:26:58,318
- I'm not angry.
- Sure, you are.

546
00:26:58,985 --> 00:27:01,071
Letting it out would be helpful,
I guarantee it.

547
00:27:01,446 --> 00:27:04,282
Shout. Scream.
Hit something, preferably not me.

548
00:27:04,574 --> 00:27:05,659
It'll do you wonders.

549
00:27:05,909 --> 00:27:08,411
You only think you know
all the answers, Patrick.

550
00:27:08,829 --> 00:27:09,871
You don't.

551
00:27:12,582 --> 00:27:13,667
You're getting a divorce.

552
00:27:14,042 --> 00:27:15,502
And now your husband is acting out.

553
00:27:15,669 --> 00:27:17,629
He just showed up
and left the kids with you.

554
00:27:20,841 --> 00:27:22,384
How'd you know
I was getting a divorce?

555
00:27:22,509 --> 00:27:24,594
Your posture.
The look in your eyes.

556
00:27:24,928 --> 00:27:26,388
Your wedding ring, it's gone.

557
00:27:28,890 --> 00:27:30,350
You've been having trouble
for years.

558
00:27:31,601 --> 00:27:33,270
Trying to make it work for the kids.

559
00:27:34,312 --> 00:27:36,857
Plus, uh, everyone in the office knows.

560
00:27:37,065 --> 00:27:39,568
Water cooler talk.
Depends on who you're speaking with

561
00:27:39,734 --> 00:27:41,319
you cheated on him or he cheated on you

562
00:27:41,486 --> 00:27:43,113
- and you've just had enough, so...
- Okay.

563
00:27:43,238 --> 00:27:44,281
Okay.

564
00:27:45,490 --> 00:27:48,285
Let's keep the focus
on the business at hand, okay?

565
00:27:50,453 --> 00:27:52,497
Why would Munroe first kill Loomis,
and then Attwood?

566
00:27:52,622 --> 00:27:54,040
What's, what's the connection?

567
00:27:54,833 --> 00:27:56,918
Mm. My question is this...

568
00:27:57,502 --> 00:27:58,712
why did the killer shoot Loomis

569
00:27:58,837 --> 00:28:00,881
and then drive the body
all the way to the creek?

570
00:28:02,549 --> 00:28:05,760
Well, not to hide the body.
He left it in plain sight, so...

571
00:28:06,887 --> 00:28:08,680
He didn't want the body found
at the kill site.

572
00:28:08,847 --> 00:28:10,682
Exactly. Why not?

573
00:28:11,600 --> 00:28:14,019
Van Pelt and Rigsby
are gonna pick up Scott Munroe.

574
00:28:15,770 --> 00:28:16,813
We'll ask him.

575
00:28:21,985 --> 00:28:23,028
Club soda, please.

576
00:28:28,366 --> 00:28:29,784
How about something
a little stronger?

577
00:28:30,619 --> 00:28:31,661
I'm buying.

578
00:28:32,037 --> 00:28:34,956
Oh, hey. Scott, right?
Scott Munroe?

579
00:28:35,874 --> 00:28:36,917
Yeah.

580
00:28:37,167 --> 00:28:38,877
That's funny, 'cause
I've been looking for you.

581
00:28:40,962 --> 00:28:42,005
Later, bitch.

582
00:28:42,672 --> 00:28:45,425
- What the hell?
- What? She's a cop.

583
00:28:46,927 --> 00:28:47,969
Yeah. Me, too.

584
00:28:48,345 --> 00:28:49,387
Up against the wall.

585
00:28:50,931 --> 00:28:52,182
Put your hands
behind your back.

586
00:28:56,019 --> 00:28:57,979
- Theresa?
- Yes, Mimi?

587
00:28:58,146 --> 00:29:00,815
- I'm done with my math homework.
- Good work.

588
00:29:01,191 --> 00:29:03,652
- Did you check it?
- No.

589
00:29:03,818 --> 00:29:05,403
- Check it, please.
- Okay.

590
00:29:05,570 --> 00:29:08,073
- After homework, can we watch TV?
- Nope.

591
00:29:08,281 --> 00:29:09,783
Hi. We got Monroe.

592
00:29:10,909 --> 00:29:13,078
You two stay here.
After you finish your homework

593
00:29:13,203 --> 00:29:15,163
you can have another snack,
they're on my desk.

594
00:29:15,330 --> 00:29:16,373
Need some help?

595
00:29:17,123 --> 00:29:18,250
And then TV?

596
00:29:18,750 --> 00:29:21,211
And then Agent Rigsby
will tell you a story.

597
00:29:26,258 --> 00:29:28,468
Mr. Munroe, tell us what happened
with Loomis and Attwood.

598
00:29:28,677 --> 00:29:30,262
I have no idea, except they're dead.

599
00:29:30,637 --> 00:29:32,472
Yeah, Attwood was dead
on the floor of your trailer.

600
00:29:32,764 --> 00:29:34,933
Man, this is a frame-up.
Maybe I should be talking to my lawyer.

601
00:29:35,183 --> 00:29:37,269
That's your right, of course.

602
00:29:37,936 --> 00:29:39,604
But this doesn't need to be adversarial.

603
00:29:40,313 --> 00:29:43,275
- Just clear a few things up for us.
- What do you wanna know?

604
00:29:43,692 --> 00:29:45,360
We're told you're the top thief
in Hadley.

605
00:29:46,236 --> 00:29:47,988
You specialize
in gold mining equipment.

606
00:29:48,655 --> 00:29:50,282
We hear you're the best at it.

607
00:29:50,615 --> 00:29:52,784
It's good to be the best
at something, right, Scott?

608
00:29:53,702 --> 00:29:55,787
That stuff holds up
its resale value real well.

609
00:29:56,121 --> 00:29:57,914
And you killed Attwood because
he found out you killed Loomis.

610
00:29:58,039 --> 00:30:00,083
You killed Loomis because he found out
you stole his equipment.

611
00:30:00,208 --> 00:30:02,127
Hey, you know
who stole Loomis' equipment?

612
00:30:02,836 --> 00:30:04,254
His own kid, Jeff.

613
00:30:04,462 --> 00:30:06,548
Yeah. I'm blowing your mind, right?

614
00:30:07,007 --> 00:30:08,883
The kid came to me last week
to unload the stuff.

615
00:30:09,509 --> 00:30:11,052
I bought the whole lot
for a hundred bucks.

616
00:30:11,636 --> 00:30:12,929
Cold little bastard.

617
00:30:13,388 --> 00:30:14,431
Call Hightower.

618
00:30:18,685 --> 00:30:20,812
Your father's mining equipment
was recently stolen.

619
00:30:20,937 --> 00:30:22,772
- Do you know anything about that?
- No.

620
00:30:24,024 --> 00:30:26,192
So it wasn't you who stole
your father's equipment?

621
00:30:26,901 --> 00:30:28,903
- No. It's not true.
- Oh, come on, Jeff.

622
00:30:29,029 --> 00:30:30,905
You were mad at him.
We get it.

623
00:30:31,239 --> 00:30:32,365
I wasn't mad at him.

624
00:30:32,782 --> 00:30:34,868
He cleaned out your college fund
and you weren't mad at him?

625
00:30:34,993 --> 00:30:37,245
That's a... little odd, Jeff.

626
00:30:37,370 --> 00:30:39,414
Talk to us, Jeff.
Help us make this right.

627
00:30:45,545 --> 00:30:47,255
My grandparents gave me
that money.

628
00:30:48,256 --> 00:30:50,050
If he had just asked me,
I would've said sure

629
00:30:50,216 --> 00:30:51,259
but he tried to hide it from me.

630
00:30:51,384 --> 00:30:54,346
So you got even by stealing
his panning equipment?

631
00:30:55,138 --> 00:30:57,766
- Did your father find out?
- Oh, he knew it was Jeff all along.

632
00:30:57,891 --> 00:30:59,392
That's why he didn't call
the police.

633
00:31:01,561 --> 00:31:03,021
Did you two get into a fight?

634
00:31:03,938 --> 00:31:05,065
Jeff, what happened?

635
00:31:07,400 --> 00:31:08,443
What happened?

636
00:31:11,780 --> 00:31:14,491
He wasn't even mad.
He said he was sorry...

637
00:31:15,200 --> 00:31:16,993
about the college money,
about everything.

638
00:31:17,118 --> 00:31:18,536
He promised to make it up to me.

639
00:31:20,330 --> 00:31:22,207
I didn't wanna listen to him,
so I walked out.

640
00:31:23,208 --> 00:31:24,959
And that was the last time
we ever spoke.

641
00:31:25,710 --> 00:31:26,753
Well, that's too bad.

642
00:31:28,213 --> 00:31:32,217
But I imagine owning
a gold mine will ease the guilt.

643
00:31:32,384 --> 00:31:33,760
Do you have a map of the area?

644
00:31:34,969 --> 00:31:36,721
- Huh?
- A map.

645
00:31:37,806 --> 00:31:40,892
I think the "Huh?"
was for the gold mine part.

646
00:31:41,142 --> 00:31:42,727
Ah. Yes.

647
00:31:42,936 --> 00:31:46,606
Uh, Jeff, what did your dad
stop doing recently?

648
00:31:49,067 --> 00:31:50,151
Washing himself?

649
00:31:51,152 --> 00:31:53,279
No. Something else.
Uh...

650
00:31:53,446 --> 00:31:55,615
What did your dad stop
saying recently?

651
00:31:56,282 --> 00:31:57,951
He stopped saying...

652
00:31:58,410 --> 00:31:59,869
that he found a gold mine.

653
00:32:00,787 --> 00:32:04,874
Yes. Which could only mean
that he found a gold mine.

654
00:32:06,084 --> 00:32:07,210
- Really?
- Really.

655
00:32:07,627 --> 00:32:09,254
And Jeff is gonna tell us where it is.

656
00:32:10,171 --> 00:32:11,714
I am? How?

657
00:32:15,009 --> 00:32:16,386
Hey, guys. Can I help ya?

658
00:32:16,636 --> 00:32:19,806
Yes. Uh, we need to look at a large-scale
map of the hill country.

659
00:32:20,265 --> 00:32:22,642
- Got one right there on the table.
- Okay. Thank you.

660
00:32:28,440 --> 00:32:29,941
Your father found gold, Jeff.

661
00:32:30,608 --> 00:32:32,444
He knew you and Cheryl
wouldn't believe him.

662
00:32:32,694 --> 00:32:34,404
He'd disappointed you
so many times before

663
00:32:34,529 --> 00:32:36,948
so he wouldn't tell anyone, not a soul.

664
00:32:37,115 --> 00:32:39,951
Not until he had that real gold
to put in your hand.

665
00:32:40,743 --> 00:32:43,121
He told you nothing. He wanted to.

666
00:32:44,080 --> 00:32:46,708
Badly. And subconsciously, he did.

667
00:32:46,833 --> 00:32:49,919
Subconsciously, he told you
exactly where that gold is.

668
00:32:50,420 --> 00:32:52,547
- So where is it?
- Yeah, where is it?

669
00:32:54,549 --> 00:32:56,384
Honestly, I don't have any idea.

670
00:32:56,718 --> 00:32:58,386
You and your father
were close, weren't you?

671
00:32:58,761 --> 00:33:00,054
You loved one another?

672
00:33:00,972 --> 00:33:02,015
- Yes.
- Yes.

673
00:33:02,599 --> 00:33:03,808
You had a connection.

674
00:33:05,977 --> 00:33:08,021
You know a lot more
than you think you know.

675
00:33:11,232 --> 00:33:12,317
Let's take a look, shall we?

676
00:33:13,359 --> 00:33:14,402
Give me your hand.

677
00:33:16,696 --> 00:33:18,239
This is for your father. Trust me.

678
00:33:18,907 --> 00:33:21,242
I need a physical connection to get
a good reading, please.

679
00:33:21,367 --> 00:33:22,535
- All right.
- Thank you.

680
00:33:23,244 --> 00:33:24,954
Now we're working together.
That's nice.

681
00:33:25,371 --> 00:33:26,414
Now, just relax.

682
00:33:28,166 --> 00:33:29,792
- Relax.
- All right.

683
00:33:30,460 --> 00:33:33,171
What I'm doing is scanning
his unconscious

684
00:33:33,296 --> 00:33:34,631
measuring his affinity.

685
00:33:35,715 --> 00:33:37,759
Narrow it down...

686
00:33:39,052 --> 00:33:40,887
You narrow it down...

687
00:33:42,972 --> 00:33:45,433
To a more specific area.

688
00:33:55,193 --> 00:33:56,236
Uh-huh.

689
00:33:57,070 --> 00:33:58,112
Interesting.

690
00:33:59,322 --> 00:34:01,074
- What is?
- Jane?

691
00:34:04,452 --> 00:34:05,495
Yes.

692
00:34:06,955 --> 00:34:07,997
Nope.

693
00:34:09,207 --> 00:34:11,042
Never mind. Didn't work.

694
00:34:12,585 --> 00:34:16,422
You said, "Uh-huh. Interesting."

695
00:34:16,589 --> 00:34:21,010
Yes, I did. Uh, I thought
I was getting a location, but, uh...

696
00:34:21,511 --> 00:34:22,554
I was wrong.

697
00:34:24,347 --> 00:34:26,140
It's not an exact science, what I do.

698
00:34:26,266 --> 00:34:27,392
No kidding.

699
00:34:27,642 --> 00:34:30,436
Well, uh... I need
to go and get me some eggs.

700
00:34:31,062 --> 00:34:33,189
- But what about the gold mine?
- Disappointing, I know

701
00:34:33,314 --> 00:34:35,149
but, uh, you seem
like a smart young man.

702
00:34:35,275 --> 00:34:36,609
I-I think you'll do fine.

703
00:34:40,780 --> 00:34:41,823
Jane!

704
00:34:43,866 --> 00:34:44,909
Jane!

705
00:34:48,121 --> 00:34:49,163
Hey!

706
00:34:50,290 --> 00:34:52,917
Jane! Jane!

707
00:36:10,868 --> 00:36:11,911
Hi, Dean.

708
00:36:12,870 --> 00:36:15,247
Mr. Jane. I'm impressed.

709
00:36:16,123 --> 00:36:17,166
You found it.

710
00:36:18,000 --> 00:36:20,628
You really did scan
that kid's unconscious, or whatever.

711
00:36:20,961 --> 00:36:22,505
No, that'd be magic.

712
00:36:23,297 --> 00:36:24,340
So, then, how?

713
00:36:24,590 --> 00:36:27,259
Well, I watched you watch
the boy's hand on the map

714
00:36:27,426 --> 00:36:28,469
and got a general direction

715
00:36:28,594 --> 00:36:30,930
then I waited for your truck
to go by and I followed you here.

716
00:36:31,347 --> 00:36:33,015
It was you that led me to the gold.

717
00:36:33,891 --> 00:36:34,934
Don't feel bad.

718
00:36:35,393 --> 00:36:37,561
I assure you, better men
than you have been taken.

719
00:36:40,398 --> 00:36:41,524
How'd you know it was me?

720
00:36:42,274 --> 00:36:44,068
Rick Loomis preferred smooth jazz.

721
00:36:44,944 --> 00:36:46,612
- You like the heavy metal.
- So?

722
00:36:46,821 --> 00:36:51,659
So your disdain for mellow saxophone
solos has cost you dearly.

723
00:36:53,369 --> 00:36:55,162
Did Rick Loomis tell you
he'd found a seam

724
00:36:55,371 --> 00:36:57,707
or did you just follow him
on the off chance?

725
00:36:58,082 --> 00:37:00,126
Look, Mr. Jane, with all due respect

726
00:37:00,584 --> 00:37:03,379
my family has been up here
in Hadley mining

727
00:37:03,504 --> 00:37:04,922
since the first gold rush.

728
00:37:06,090 --> 00:37:09,969
"That gold belongs to me
to my family."

729
00:37:11,220 --> 00:37:12,847
"Not to some newbie like Loomis."

730
00:37:27,403 --> 00:37:30,156
Poor Wes Attwood. He figured out
the killer was you, didn't he?

731
00:37:31,157 --> 00:37:33,159
Tried to blackmail you,
get a cut of that gold.

732
00:37:33,492 --> 00:37:35,077
Hey, you believe the nerve
of that guy?

733
00:37:36,078 --> 00:37:37,121
Greedy bastard.

734
00:37:39,123 --> 00:37:40,666
You do hear the irony there, right?

735
00:37:41,125 --> 00:37:43,961
Yeah, when I heard you cops
were lookin' for Scott Munroe

736
00:37:45,504 --> 00:37:46,547
that made it easy.

737
00:37:47,423 --> 00:37:49,884
I make it look like Munroe killed Attwood

738
00:37:50,801 --> 00:37:52,553
and I'm off the hook for all of it.

739
00:37:52,928 --> 00:37:53,971
Splendid plan.

740
00:37:56,348 --> 00:38:00,144
The only thing standing between me
and my gold now, Mr. Jane...

741
00:38:01,145 --> 00:38:03,189
- is you.
- Any time, Madeleine.

742
00:38:03,981 --> 00:38:05,107
Drop it, Puttock!

743
00:38:05,441 --> 00:38:07,902
No, screw that! You drop yours.

744
00:38:08,068 --> 00:38:09,111
Drop it!

745
00:38:16,827 --> 00:38:18,120
Son of a bitch.

746
00:38:19,163 --> 00:38:20,206
Seriously?

747
00:38:38,474 --> 00:38:39,517
Is he dead?

748
00:38:51,153 --> 00:38:52,196
Yeah.

749
00:38:57,034 --> 00:38:58,494
You know, I should applaud
your bravery

750
00:38:58,619 --> 00:39:01,914
but I gotta say, practically speaking
I-I-I...

751
00:39:02,414 --> 00:39:04,583
What were you thinking?
You coulda killed me!

752
00:39:05,417 --> 00:39:06,460
I'm sorry.

753
00:39:07,002 --> 00:39:08,712
I got mad.
I didn't know what I was thinking.

754
00:39:10,589 --> 00:39:12,049
That was some good shooting, though.
That...

755
00:39:12,550 --> 00:39:15,302
Leg shot that took him down
was v-very precise.

756
00:39:18,097 --> 00:39:19,515
I was aiming for his head.

757
00:39:20,140 --> 00:39:21,433
Oh, fantastic.

758
00:39:31,986 --> 00:39:33,654
- Hey. Hey, Jeff.
- Hey.

759
00:39:34,071 --> 00:39:35,322
I hear your mom's flying in.

760
00:39:35,656 --> 00:39:37,658
Yeah, from Fresno.
I'm picking her up in the morning.

761
00:39:38,617 --> 00:39:40,160
Kinda nervous about it.
It's been a while.

762
00:39:40,286 --> 00:39:42,746
Buy her some flowers.
Moms like flowers.

763
00:39:43,622 --> 00:39:45,124
And you can afford a good bunch.

764
00:39:45,666 --> 00:39:48,043
We had that sent over from
the Bureau of Land Management.

765
00:39:48,669 --> 00:39:51,672
You father was in the process
of filing a mineral rights claim

766
00:39:51,797 --> 00:39:53,257
to the area where he struck gold.

767
00:39:53,382 --> 00:39:55,384
He named you and Cheryl
as co-owners.

768
00:39:56,677 --> 00:39:58,053
You own half a gold mine.

769
00:40:00,723 --> 00:40:01,765
Wow, I...

770
00:40:03,225 --> 00:40:04,894
You father came through in the end.

771
00:40:07,938 --> 00:40:10,774
- Thanks for, uh... you know.
- You're welcome.

772
00:40:11,150 --> 00:40:13,819
Hey, uh, will you, um...
thank Mr. Jane for me?

773
00:40:14,361 --> 00:40:16,780
Will do. Will do.
Good luck to you, Jeff.

774
00:40:17,615 --> 00:40:18,657
Thanks.

775
00:40:33,005 --> 00:40:34,381
I hope they weren't too much trouble.

776
00:40:34,673 --> 00:40:38,385
Not at all.
I had three nearly feral brothers.

777
00:40:38,844 --> 00:40:39,970
Your kids are sweethearts.

778
00:40:49,605 --> 00:40:50,648
Thank you.

779
00:40:51,106 --> 00:40:52,149
Hey...

780
00:40:52,816 --> 00:40:53,943
Not a problem.

781
00:40:58,405 --> 00:40:59,573
Let me get 'em outta your hair.

782
00:41:04,119 --> 00:41:06,538
Hello. Hello.

783
00:41:06,956 --> 00:41:08,248
Get up! Get up!

784
00:41:08,916 --> 00:41:10,042
Come on.

785
00:41:11,919 --> 00:41:14,588
Give me some sugar.
Give me some sugar.

786
00:41:18,175 --> 00:41:19,218
Hey.

787
00:41:19,885 --> 00:41:20,928
Hey.

788
00:41:21,679 --> 00:41:22,805
It was okay, then?

789
00:41:24,181 --> 00:41:25,224
Case closed.

790
00:41:27,726 --> 00:41:28,769
What was it like?

791
00:41:29,728 --> 00:41:30,771
"It"?

792
00:41:31,647 --> 00:41:32,690
You know...

793
00:41:34,650 --> 00:41:35,693
Oh.

794
00:41:36,151 --> 00:41:37,820
You mean going out
with another woman?

795
00:41:38,112 --> 00:41:39,154
Yeah. No.

796
00:41:39,530 --> 00:41:40,781
Uh, come on.
You know what I mean.

797
00:41:41,031 --> 00:41:42,074
It was great.

798
00:41:43,325 --> 00:41:46,370
It's fun, intense, um, challenging.

799
00:41:46,620 --> 00:41:47,830
She's super dynamic.

800
00:41:49,081 --> 00:41:51,458
Yeah. I think we had
a great connection.

801
00:41:52,126 --> 00:41:53,168
That's great.

802
00:41:54,503 --> 00:41:55,796
What, you a little jealous?

